WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.640
Have you ever wondered

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
if there are more natural ways
to lower your high blood pressure,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.520
guard against Alzheimer's,
lose weight, and feel better?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.720
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.840
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.280
Today, we look at different ways
to get the benefits of GLP-1,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.560
with natural Ozempic alternatives.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.920
I’ve talked about the upsides

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.560
of pharmacologically boosting
GLP-1 activity

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.760
in terms of weight loss,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.560
given the ravages of obesity,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.000
and I’ve also discussed
its downsides,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.240
including common
gastrointestinal reactions

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.600
and muscle loss,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.520
as well as the rarer
and more speculative

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.280
side effects
such as thyroid cancer,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.760
pancreatitis,
and pancreatic cancer.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.560
And I’ve summarized
the cost-benefit trade-off,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.840
detailing for whom the benefits
may far outweigh the risks,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.440
at least in the short term.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.080
But is there any way
to get the benefits of GLP-1

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.200
without the risks,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.200
by boosting it naturally
with diet and lifestyle changes?

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.400
Well, there’s exercise.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.120
It appears
to acutely increase GLP-1.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.600
There have been five studies
of exercise and GLP-1,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.040
and four out of five of them

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.680
found higher concentrations
of total GLP-1

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.920
after High Intensity
Interval Training

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.520
and Sprint Interval Training

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.320
as well as Moderate Intensity
Continuous Training,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.880
with no apparent difference
between High Intensity Interval

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.920
and Moderate Intensity
Continuous.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.360
Compared to control groups

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.920
where the study subjects
didn’t exercise at all,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.920
both types of physical activity

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.040
(High Intensity Interval and
Moderate Intensity Continuous)

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.240
induced significantly higher
GLP-1 concentrations

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.760
immediately after exercise,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.080
and 30-90 minutes later.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.960
Unfortunately, there appeared
to be publication bias,

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.080
meaning some trials
that failed to show an effect

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.680
may have been quietly shelved,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.760
thereby skewing the results.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.480
But what’s the downside
of exercising?

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.160
The evidence is overwhelming

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.840
that nearly everyone
can benefit

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.040
from becoming
more physically active.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.200
What about diet?

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.120
There are a variety
of natural products,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.480
foods, beverages, and spices,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.800
that have been shown to boost
our own natural GLP-1 levels.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.880
But can endogenous GLP-1,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.440
meaning GLP-1
from inside the body,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.640
work as an alternative

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.000
to the drugs that mimic GLP-1,
like Ozempic?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:16.800
If you haven’t yet,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.960
I recommend watching
or re-watching my video

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.440
about what GLP-1 is
and how it works,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.240
to best fully grasp
the issue at hand.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.960
Our natural GLP-1 levels
rise and fall

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.720
in response to meal ingestion.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.480
When we take
these drugs, though,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.920
levels in our blood remain
relatively constant, and high.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.280
But "high" here
is a bit of an understatement.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.240
The total concentration

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.720
of these GLP-1 lookalike drugs
in our bloodstream

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.480
is typically around
20-30 nanomoles per liter.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.880
Okay, here’s how much GLP-1

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.640
rises naturally
in our bloodstream

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.560
in the two hours
after we eat a meal,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.480
up to maybe 20 or 30 picomoles.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.080
"Nano-" means billionth;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.560
"pico-" means trillionth.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.920
So, at this scale,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.200
drug levels are up
to like 25,000.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.200
How could
boosting natural levels

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.720
even come close?

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.120
Well, part of the discrepancy
may be an artifact

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.360
of taking blood
from people’s veins

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.720
to do blood tests.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.520
Since natural GLP-1
is rapidly deactivated

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.120
by an enzyme in our blood,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.440
by the time GLP-1
is secreted by our gut,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.920
makes it through our liver,
heart, and lungs,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.520
then out into our arteries,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.280
only to later reach the veins

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.600
where the lab tech
is pulling out blood,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.720
the levels we see are lower
than the arterial levels

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.800
that GLP-1 receptors
are exposed to

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.080
throughout our body,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.560
though only
by a relatively small amount.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.080
But how do drugs like Ozempic

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.800
sit around in the body
for weeks instead of minutes?

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.240
They are chemically modified

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.000
to stick to the most abundant
protein in our blood.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.720
So, even though the total
drug level in our blood

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.640
is up in the nanomolar range,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.720
it’s so tightly bound to protein

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.880
that the active level
in our blood

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.680
that’s free to actually interact
with tissue receptors

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.600
is like a hundred times smaller,

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.400
at least down
in the picomolar range.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.920
Thus, although at first glance,

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.520
it appears
that plasma drug levels

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.360
are extremely high,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.480
they actually may be closer
to those of natural GLP-1 levels

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.400
after strong stimulation.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.080
But still, there seems
to be a huge discrepancy.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.800
It’s GLP-1 activation
in the brain

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.840
that appears to account for
the regulation of food intake,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.360
but it isn’t currently clear

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.200
how this might be related
to GLP-1 in the blood.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.680
The primary source
of GLP-1 secretion

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.760
in the body is the gut.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.040
So, wouldn’t it get eaten up
by enzymes in the blood

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.920
before much of it
even made it into the brain?

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.560
It can cross
the blood-brain barrier,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.360
but is enough
really getting up there?

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.360
Well, GLP-1 is also produced

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.920
by nerves
in the brainstem itself,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.120
and a large body of evidence

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.720
points to
the appetite-reducing effects

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.040
of GLP-1 mimicking drugs,
like Ozempic,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.800
being mediated by
these brain GLP-1 receptors.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.360
So, how could
natural blood levels,

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.520
expected after
dietary interventions,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.240
aiming to increase
the secretion of gut GLP-1

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.800
still have
clinically relevant effects?

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.440
Yes, the GLP-1 released
from our intestinal cells

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.960
is rapidly degraded,
leading to low systemic levels,

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.040
but its effects
appear to be mediated

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.320
by vagus nerve stimulation.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.280
The vagus nerve
connects the gut

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.200
straight up
into the brainstem,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.160
so it’s directly wired
to the brain

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.680
and doesn’t have to rely
on blood transport.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.480
But wait, there are GLP-1
receptors on the pancreas,

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.240
the kidneys,
the liver, and the heart.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.200
Why would the body
have GLP-1 receptors

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.800
on our peripheral organs,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.320
if the hormone acts mainly
through the vagus nerve?

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.240
It’s possible
that strong stimuli

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.000
can boost levels
high enough in the blood

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.360
to activate
these peripheral receptors.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.920
So, perhaps,
the way GLP-1 normally works

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.280
is that we eat a meal,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.840
and GLP-1 sends
the appetite reducing signal

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.480
to our brain directly
through the vagus nerve,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.480
and then, our brain
relays the messages

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.040
to the relevant systems
throughout the body.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.640
Injecting people
with high levels

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.520
of a GLP-1-mimicking drug,
like Ozempic,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.240
means it can circulate
directly through the bloodstream

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.320
to all these organs,
including the brain,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.440
and a strong
dietary GLP-1 stimulus

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.520
might be able to do both.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:16.960
But you can’t really know

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.240
whether natural,
physiological levels of GLP-1

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.520
can suppress appetite,
until you...

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.680
put it to the test.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.200
When different levels of GLP-1

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.120
are dripped
straight into people’s veins,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.960
you can see
a dose-dependent reduction

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.000
in both hunger and food intake.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.280
Okay, but effective doses
used in infusion studies

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.280
quadrupled baseline levels
or more,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.160
whereas the blood levels

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.800
increased by
GLP-1-boosting foods

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.120
tended to only double
baseline levels.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.440
So, while high-dose
GLP-1 infusion

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.400
drives a clear change
in hunger and eating behaviors,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.800
is it possible
that inducing GLP-1 secretion

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.880
through diet may not?

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.720
Well, remember how food

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.480
naturally only boosts
blood levels up around 20?

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.320
It turns out you can get
a significant drop

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.440
in food intake

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:07.120
up to 30-35% less
of an all-you-can-eat meal

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.640
simply by getting your GLP-1
levels up to 10 or 15,

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.400
or even just 5!

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.160
That is well within
doable dietary ranges.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.920
So, while low natural levels

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.640
might appear to have
little effect at first glance,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.680
a slight increase in levels
by dietary manipulation

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.160
of GLP-1 secretion
could be sufficient

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.160
to have clinical relevance.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.360
Thus, we don’t have to strive

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.040
for levels comparable to those
obtained using drugs.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.520
And, of course,
this makes sense, right?

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.280
Why would our body
naturally produce

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.080
an appetite-suppressing hormone
in response to food,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.760
if it didn’t end up
suppressing our appetite?

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.320
This review on
boosting the hormone GLP-1

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.360
by natural products

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.280
concluded there are
compelling studies

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.560
that suggest that
the berberine in barberries,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.200
quercetin in capers,
red onions, and buckwheat,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.320
ginseng, ginger root, gardenia,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.040
green tea,
wheat fiber, soybeans,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.880
curcumin in turmeric, cinnamon,

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.480
and resveratrol in grapes,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.840
each have potent effects
on GLP-1 activity.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.600
The hope is that
increasing the release

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.360
of our own GLP-1 hormone

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.880
by eating specific foods

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.480
could have similar benefits
to GLP-1-mimicking drugs,

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.240
without their potentially
disabling side effects.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:23.040
But any time a review
makes a statement of fact,

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.840
like, "Potent effects
on GLP-1 activity,"

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.400
it’s always important
to dig up the original studies,

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.280
the so-called
"primary literature."

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.160
For example,
here’s the section on soy:

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:35.920
researchers demonstrated
that soybeans

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.200
can normalize blood sugars

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.280
by inducing GLP-1 secretion,

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.200
dietary consumption
of soybean protein

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:42.480
induces GLP-1

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:43.520
and, moreover,

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.200
soybean protein increases
GLP-1 expression and secretion.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.200
"This combined evidence
strongly suggests

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.600
that soybean protein
exerts modulatory effects

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.760
on GLP-1 secretion."

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.120
Sounds great!

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.520
But if you pull
these three sources,

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.640
that was based on studies
on mice, rats,

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.280
and in Petri dishes.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.200
Gardenia-derived products

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.920
also purportedly
potentiate GLP-1 secretion

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.240
based on cells and rodents,

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.800
but, when actually tested
in humans,

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.400
those in the gardenia group

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.200
actually showed
a decrease in GLP-1.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.640
Evidence also suggests ginseng
improves GLP-1 secretion,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:21.840
but again, that’s just evidence

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.960
from rats and test tubes.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.080
What about that
red wine compound, resveratrol?

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.160
Works in diabetic rats,

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.480
but not in diabetic humans.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.320
Green tea is said to increase
GLP-1 levels in actual patients,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:38.560
actual human patients!

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.320
But, in fact,
the increase over placebo

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.760
did not reach
statistical significance.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.280
Olive oil is said to induce

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.560
higher GLP-1 concentrations
than butter,

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.800
but the study that was cited,
this one,

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.160
actually showed
no significant difference.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.680
This was
on healthy subjects, though;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.520
a similar study
on those with diabetes

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:59.360
did show a larger
GLP-1 response,

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.920
though only by about 15 percent.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.120
Avocados have lots
of monounsaturated fats, too.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.280
And online,
you’ll find so-called experts

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.200
who claim that
eating avocados can be,

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.080
"as effective as getting
weight-loss injections,"

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:12.960
a food that works

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.600
"just like the miracle
weight-loss jab Ozempic,"

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.640
and that’s from a nutritionist!

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:21.200
"Guaca-mo-zempic"

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.440
may work if you’re a rat,

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:24.400
but in humans,

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.880
GLP-1 was found to be
significantly lower

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.480
in the avocado-added meal.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.400
Speaking of online speculation,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.920
what do we know about berberine,

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.080
the supplement dubbed
"Nature’s Ozempic,"

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.280
a component of barberries,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.120
a dried fruit
I’ve previously profiled

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.960
as a treatment for acne?

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.840
Well, berberine
boosts GLP-1 secretion

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.920
in rats and Petri dishes,

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.000
but there haven’t been any
GLP-1 studies on humans yet.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.680
What we care about, though,
is weight loss,

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:53.280
and there have been
a bunch of studies

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:55.200
on the effects
of berberine and barberries

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.800
on body weight.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.560
No effect was found,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.000
either for berberine supplements

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.120
or barberries themselves.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.960
This is all very disappointing.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.880
Something in the spice
"fenugreek"

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.200
seemed to boost GLP-1 signaling
in a test tube,

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.840
but there are no human studies,

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:13.680
and it didn’t even work in mice.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.680
So, fenugreek flopped,

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.880
but there are some spices

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:20.640
that can legitimately
boost GLP-1 in humans.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.320
If you have people
eat the same meal,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:26.400
but chew each bite
either 15 times or 40 times,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.200
the blood levels

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.280
of the appetite-suppressing
hormone GLP-1

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:29.920
in the blood stream

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.960
are higher
after 40 chews than 15,

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.560
and those who chewed 40 times

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.200
ended up consuming
about 75 fewer calories

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.360
than the 15-time chewers.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.360
The researchers suggest
that chewing more

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.000
may help people
keep their weight down.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.520
Okay, but who wants to sit there

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.680
and count how many times
they chew?

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.640
What if you just eat
chewier foods?

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.720
What if you have people
eat shredded cabbage,

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.240
which requires a lot of chewing,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.560
or the same amount
of pureed cabbage;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.000
same food, but requiring
different amounts of chewing?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.000
GLP-1 blood levels were higher

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.080
in the shredded cabbage
chewing group

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.960
than the pureed cabbage
non-chewing one,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.240
at 45, 60, and 90 minutes,
at least initially.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.160
The researchers were careful
to make sure

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.480
both groups ate
at the same rate,

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.040
because eating
even the same food more slowly

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.000
can result in a greater
GLP-1 response.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.520
Researchers had people

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.840
eat the same amount of ice cream

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.400
over a period of either
five minutes or 30 minutes,

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:28.160
and those who ate
the exact same amount

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.920
of the same food more slowly

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.800
experienced a significant boost
in GLP-1 levels in their blood

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.240
for hours after the meal,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:37.520
about a 30 percent bump overall.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:40.080
On average, the participants
were overweight,

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.880
but a lot slimmer
than your average American.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.280
What about essentially
re-doing the same study,

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:46.960
but with obese individuals

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.840
who could really use
the extra GLP-1 boost?

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.890
And here we go.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:53.840
The researchers found
disparate results

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.080
when they tested
obese adolescents

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.680
versus obese adults.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.560
With the obese teens,
researchers saw the same outcome

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.160
as they did with
the overweight adults;

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.560
a significantly higher
GLP-1 response

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.240
after slower eating.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.200
But the rate of eating

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.080
didn’t seem to matter
in obese adults.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:11.200
Same thing with satiety.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.960
Obese adolescents felt fuller,

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.840
more satiated for longer
when eating more slowly,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.680
but speed of eating
didn’t seem to matter

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.080
in the adults with obesity.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:21.280
But, at least for some,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.120
more chewing
and eating more slowly

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.320
may raise GLP-1 levels,
no matter what’s consumed.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.760
But are there certain foods
that specifically boost GLP-1?

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.560
There are a few
spices that might.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.680
Volunteers were served
rice with vegetable curry

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:38.080
made with three different
doses of spices.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.040
The bland control meal
had no spices at all,

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.200
just tomato puree with eggplant.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.480
The low-spice meal added
a tablespoon of curry spices,

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.160
plus onions, garlic, and ginger.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.640
And the third variation,
the high-spice curry,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.880
had double the spices,
two tablespoons.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:56.560
The spices were turmeric,
coriander seeds, cumin seeds,

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.200
dried Indian gooseberry powder--
also known as amla--

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.360
cayenne pepper,
cinnamon, and cloves

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.080
mixed in the ratio of eight
to four to four to four,

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.960
to two to one to one,
respectively.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.600
And the average bump
in GLP-1 blood concentration

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.520
for those eating
both the low-spice

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:12.560
and high-spice meals

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.360
were 17 and 32 percent higher,

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.400
compared to the bland
control meal without the spices.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.240
About the same calories
and macronutrients,

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.000
but the spicier meals
raised GLP-1 levels higher.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.840
But which spice was it?

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.320
Ginger compounds boosts
GLP-1 in mice but not rats,

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:33.440
and in humans, we appeared
to react more like the rats:

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.400
no effect.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.520
For many of the other spices,
there isn’t even in vitro data,

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.440
but rather only in silico,

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.360
meaning just some kind
of computer modeling

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:44.560
that didn’t actually test
anything in a biological system.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:46.320
But we do have data on curcumin,

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:49.280
the yellow compound
found in the spice turmeric.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.840
This study used
180 milligrams of curcumin,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.120
which is the amount found in
a single teaspoon of turmeric.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.600
The researchers also tested
fish oil supplements,

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.160
but while the fish oil failed,
the curcumin

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.160
reduced the blood sugar spike
after a meal,

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:06.320
which is something both
GLP-1 and GLP-1 drugs can do,

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:09.120
though GLP-1 levels
weren’t directly measured.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.760
The researchers suggest
it may be a GLP-1 effect,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.400
since curcumin
stimulates GLP-1 in rodents,

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.000
as well as cells
in a Petri dish,

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.040
but there hasn’t been
a human study until now.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.720
Six months
of curcumin supplementation

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.880
led to a quadrupling
of GLP-1 levels,

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.720
compared to placebo.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.720
Okay, so, turmeric may help.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.960
What about cinnamon?

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.760
Scandinavian researchers
gave people rice pudding

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.040
with and without
one or three grams of cinnamon,

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:38.240
which is about
a third of a teaspoon,

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.600
or a full teaspoon.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.760
And those getting
the full teaspoon of cinnamon

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.320
more than doubled
the GLP-1 bump-from-baseline

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:47.200
compared to the control pudding
without the cinnamon.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.600
So, that’s another spice
that may work,

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.240
though people didn’t report
feeling any more satiated

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:55.520
eating the cinnamon-y pudding
compared to plain.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:59.120
And finally, the third spice
to boost GLP-1 in humans?

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:00.880
Cayenne pepper.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:02.320
What happened
when a half a teaspoon

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.160
of cayenne pepper
was added to a meal?

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.360
Over a short period of time--
15 minutes--

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:09.520
a single spicy meal
significantly increased

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:11.520
blood levels of GLP-1.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:12.880
Though,
like in the cinnamon study,

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:16.400
this did not appear to translate
into them feeling any fuller,

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:18.000
but the researcher
didn’t measure

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.320
actual subsequent food intake.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.720
Most of the poisons
used by humans

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.000
come from bitter plants.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.240
For example,
strychnine and hemlock.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:27.280
At the same time,

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.200
some of the most
popular beverages

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.480
in the world are bitter,

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.960
as are some
of the healthiest foods.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.600
Bitter taste receptors
aren’t only on our tongue,

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:37.840
but throughout much
of our digestive tract,

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:40.720
on the very same cells
lining our gut that secrete

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:43.520
the appetite-suppressing
hormone, GLP-1.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:45.760
And indeed,
a number of bitter compounds

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:47.440
have been shown
to potently stimulate

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:49.280
the release of hormones
like GLP-1

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.680
in Petri dishes and lab animals.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.360
What about people?

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:54.560
And beer!

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:56.800
Bee is one of the most popular
bitter beverages,

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.800
thanks to hops.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.200
Given that bitter herbs
have evidently been used

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.920
in times of scarcity
to reduce hunger,

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:06.960
researchers tested low and high
doses of hops extracts

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.360
on people undergoing
a 24-hour fast,

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.160
and found both doses seemed
to lower feelings of hunger

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:13.920
better than placebo.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:16.320
Might that be a GLP-1 effect?

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.080
Let’s find out.

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:20.560
Taking a hops extract
before a meal,

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:23.440
in either immediate
or delayed-release capsules,

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:25.840
led to significantly more
GLP-1 release

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.680
than did a placebo.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:28.720
And, more importantly,

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.240
when the study participants
were given

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.640
all-you-can-eat ham sandwiches
a few hours later,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.960
they ate significantly less.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:37.840
They didn’t feel significantly
less hungry or more full,

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:41.120
but, overall, still ate
about 200 calories less.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:43.440
So, a bitter hop extract

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.600
significantly decreased
calorie intake,

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.760
while increasing
appetite-suppressing hormones,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.680
like GLP-1.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:51.920
These changes occurred
without the participants

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:53.760
feeling any different,
appetite-wise,

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.240
or liking the sandwiches
any less.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.800
Now, the extract
did make them feel

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:01.120
nauseated, bloat-y, and barf-y,

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:02.800
but maybe
that’s just more evidence

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:04.400
it might be working
through GLP-1,

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:05.600
since those are
common side effects

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:08.480
of the GLP-1-mimicking drugs,
like Ozempic.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.600
Well, wait,
no wonder they ate less,

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:14.640
but evidently, the degree
of gastrointestinal discomfort

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:18.320
did not correlate
with how much less they ate.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:20.720
The main reason
I’d caution against hops

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.280
is because of a compound in hops
called 8-PN

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:24.560
that I’ve talked about before

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.920
in a two-part video series.

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:29.920
What about quinine,

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.840
perhaps the best-studied
bitter substance

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.360
in human history?

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.480
Extracted from the bark
of the cinchona tree,

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:37.600
quinine was not only

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:41.520
the first effective treatment
for malaria 400 years ago,

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.680
but, remarkably,
remains a common and effective

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.360
treatment to this day.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.000
And give someone
275 milligrams of it,

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.040
and GLP-1 secretion
is stimulated.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:53.840
Enough to actually reduce
appetite and body weight?

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:57.280
"Dietary quinine reduces
body weight and food intake,"

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:58.640
in rats.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:00.480
What about in people?

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:02.880
"Intragastric
quinine administration

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.200
decreases hedonic eating,"

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:06.640
meaning eating for pleasure,

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.160
even if you aren’t
really hungry.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.400
In this case,
a chocolate milkshake.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:11.760
And if you’re thinking,
"Well, of course

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.360
they’d drink less milkshake

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:16.160
if they just had something
super bitter," but, no.

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:18.000
The quinine was administered

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:20.000
directly into their stomach
through a tube,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.040
so it bypassed the bitter
receptors in their mouth.

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:24.560
There are still
those bitter receptors

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:25.520
in our gut, though,

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.080
that we aren’t even
consciously aware of.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.080
The researchers
even did brain scans,

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:32.240
and concluded that
being unknowingly slipped

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:33.920
about 200 milligrams of quinine

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.160
not only affected
how much the study participants

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:37.600
felt like they wanted to eat,

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.440
but how much
they actually did eat,

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:42.040
by interfering
with reward brain circuits.

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:46.000
And you don’t have to go
lick some tree bark to get it.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.000
It’s available
in every grocery store

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:51.120
in the form of tonic water.

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:54.560
A liter of tonic water has about
60 to 70 milligrams of quinine,

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:57.440
and bitter lemon,
another popular cocktail mixer,

00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:58.720
has about half that.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:00.960
But getting
200 milligrams of quinine

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.000
still would be like drinking
three quarts of tonic water.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.920
Can we get away with less?

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:06.400
Yes.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:07.600
In this study, people were given

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.000
just 18 milligrams of quinine,
or placebo,

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.600
and then, an hour later,
everyone was given

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.920
all-you-can-eat
ham and cheese sandwiches.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.400
And caloric intake
was significantly lower

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:19.440
after the quinine than placebo
by about 14 percent.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.960
So, drinking a third of a liter
of tonic water before a meal

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:26.720
could reduce your caloric intake
by 82 calories.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.400
But a third of a liter
of tonic water

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:29.840
has more than
a hundred calories!

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.400
So, that wouldn’t
do you much good.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.400
Tonic water is basically
quinine-spiked

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:36.400
carbonated sugar water.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.400
Now, there are
sugar-free varieties,

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.560
or, you could go
straight to the source.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.480
What if you got about
the same amount of quinine

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.960
in the form of powdered bark?

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.120
The effects of giving about
a third of a teaspoon a day

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:48.400
of powdered cinchona bark

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:51.360
on satiety, weight loss,
and body composition

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:54.080
in a population
of overweight adults.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:56.400
Significantly more weight loss,

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.280
significantly more waist lost,
and even slimmer hips

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.200
in the bark group,
compared to placebo,

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.880
and the weight loss
was in fat mass,

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.880
with greater
lean mass preservation,

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:06.240
compared to placebo.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:09.920
So, in humans,
quinine may boost GLP-1,

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.920
decrease hedonic hunger,

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:14.240
lower appetite,
decrease calorie intake,

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.080
and decrease body fat.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.520
But there are "risks
for the consumption of quinine."

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.280
Quinine is a drug

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:24.240
and may in fact be among
the most common causes

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.440
of severe
drug-induced disorders.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:29.760
Just the levels of quinine
found in tonic water

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.440
may cause potentially fatal

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:35.360
immunologically-mediated
hypersensitivity reactions,

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.680
basically
like allergic reactions,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.280
where it interacts
with antibodies

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:41.440
to trigger autoimmune reactions

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:44.240
that can cause chills, fever,
a drop in blood pressure,

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:46.880
painful bluish discoloration
of the hands and feet,

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:49.680
and clotting
within your blood vessels.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:50.880
It can also chew up

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:53.280
your red blood cells,
leading to anemia.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.920
Chew up your platelets, leading
to bleeding and bruising.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:57.600
And chew up
your white blood cells,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:59.280
leading to immune dysfunction.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:00.800
As well as
acute kidney injury,

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.520
the dissolution of your muscles,
liver toxicity, heart damage,

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:05.760
respiratory failure,
low blood sugars, blindness,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.200
and toxic epidermal necrolysis,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.240
which can manifest
as genital blistering,

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:16.720
sloughed penile erosion,
or an eroded scrotum.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.760
Even minute exposure to quinine
in common beverages

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:22.000
can cause
severe adverse reactions

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.160
involving multiple
organ systems.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:25.920
So, the "moral of the story"

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.880
is that a relaxing gin and tonic
may be the cause

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:31.440
of life-threatening disease.

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:34.960
Now, these are rare,
idiosyncratic reactions.

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:37.520
Just because peanuts
can be life-threatening

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:39.200
to someone with a peanut allergy

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.000
doesn’t mean everybody
needs to avoid peanuts.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.320
But there are
non-hypersensitivity

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:45.760
adverse effects, too.

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:49.520
For example, when quinine
isn’t eroding scrotums,

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.760
it may still be shrinking them.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.840
Researchers found quinine
so damaging

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:55.800
to the testicles of rats
that they suggested

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:59.920
that quinine should be explored
as male contraceptive.

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:03.600
Quinine is an extremely toxic
agent in overdose,

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:06.480
with vision loss
as a common outcome.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:09.360
The reason people may have
quinine pills around their house

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:13.120
is that some people take them
for nocturnal leg cramps.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.040
The U.S. Food and Drug
Administration concluded

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:18.320
that this was not safe;
so, it banned quinine’s use

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:20.640
for this purpose in both
over-the-counter preparations

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:22.000
and even in prescriptions,

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:23.840
after receiving
hundreds of reports

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:26.880
of serious adverse events
associated with quinine use,

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:29.120
including 93 deaths.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.120
It’s still available
with a prescription

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.720
as a malaria drug, though,

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:35.840
which doctors were
prescribing for leg cramps,

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.000
skirting FDA recommendations,

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.800
but given its rare but serious
allergic-type reactions,

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:43.360
and risk of OD’ing
on quinine pills,

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:45.840
that’s probably not a good idea.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:48.080
Thankfully,
following warnings by the FDA,

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:50.160
the use of quinine
for treatment of muscle cramps

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.400
has dramatically declined.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:54.080
Of course, even though
the FDA banned it

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:55.600
from one aisle
of the drug store,

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.680
it still may be available
in another,

00:23:57.680 --> 00:23:59.600
in the form of tonic water.

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:01.280
Yeah, but can
the lower levels of the drug

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:03.920
found in drinks
still cause problems?

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.640
Yes, an overdose
can leave you blind,

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.280
affecting not only the retina
in the back of your eye

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:12.640
but the iris in the front,
leading to oval pupils.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:14.400
How bizarre is that?

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.120
Okay, okay,
but what if you don’t overdose?

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:19.840
Can quinine
still affect your vision?

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:21.040
Following the identification

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:23.840
by the Armed Forces
Institute of Pathology

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:29.440
of quinine in autopsy specimens
from military pilot fatalities,

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:31.520
researchers had volunteers
drink tonic water

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:34.880
for two weeks and were indeed
able to show some abnormalities.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.960
Even transient blurring
of vision

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:38.800
can be disastrous if experienced

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:42.240
by anyone operating
heavy machinery at high speeds.

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.640
So, given the concern
that tonic water consumption

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:47.200
may have contributed
to aviation accidents,

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:49.200
the use of tonic water by pilots

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:52.160
is actually forbidden
three days before flying.

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.000
I didn’t know that.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.320
So, how much is too much?

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:58.720
There was a case reported
of a 46-year-old man

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.360
with increasing
visual deterioration

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.320
and difficulty with
night driving over three months,

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:05.760
during which
he had been drinking

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:08.080
a gallon of tonic water a day.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:11.120
Okay, so, a gallon
may be too much.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.600
This person was drinking
a liter of tonic water

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:15.040
prior to the onset of his

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:18.000
"Quinine water-triggered
heart arrhythmia."

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.040
His doctors suggest the public
is simply unaware of the risks

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.320
associated with excessive
consumption of tonic water,

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:28.000
but how many people are drinking
almost a quart a day?

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:31.680
The "no-untoward-effect"
level in humans

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:35.280
has been estimated to be
52.5 mg of quinine a day,

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.480
which is about a two and a half
cups (590 ml) of tonic water.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:42.240
Based on ototoxicity,
damage to our hearing,

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:43.520
maybe we shouldn’t
drink much more

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:46.560
than two cups (470 ml) a day,
but these levels are based on

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:48.880
trying to extrapolate
from animal models.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:52.160
Since quinine was so commonly
prescribed to treat leg cramps,

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.160
can’t we just look at large
populations of people

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.560
and see if anything
bad happened?

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:57.920
Yes, indeed, we can,

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.600
and those prescribed quinine
had a 27 percent increased risk

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:06.960
of acute kidney injury,
which is not as bad as death:

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:09.440
"Association Between
Long-term Quinine Exposure

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:11.120
and All-Cause Mortality."

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:13.840
Following 175,000 people
for about six years,

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:15.600
researchers found
that those who were taking

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:17.920
about 200 mg of quinine a day

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:21.680
had a 25 percent
greater risk of dying.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.360
Those on around 300 mg

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:25.200
had an 83 percent
greater risk of dying,

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.640
and those on 400 mg or more

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:29.360
appeared to double
their risk for death.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:33.440
So, I would recommend
staying away from quinine.

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:35.920
Although the FDA has determined
that carbonated beverages

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:39.600
may safely contain up to 83 mg
of quinine per liter,

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:42.720
bottles of bubbly drinks usually
don’t say how much is in them,

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:44.960
and some popular brands
don’t even list it

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:46.720
as an ingredient at all.

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:49.760
How do you know if your drink
has quinine or not?

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.600
Hold it up to a black light,

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.400
and if it lights up
with an iridescent blue glow,

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:57.600
well, quinine
is the secret ingredient

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:02.400
for making
fluorescent Jell-O shots.

